graphic order since they're running in a pipe. For mixed case like \"Banana\", \"Cherry\", \"apple\", \"date\", the C locale uses ASCII ordering where uppercase comes first, so I expect both to output them in that order. And for the dot-files test, I need to verify both binaries handle hidden files consistently.\n\nNow I need to write the regex pattern for normalizing the ls binary name in the test utilities, similar to how the hostname test handles different paths and optional prefixes.\n\nWriting test setup code... Let me verify the regex handles edge cases correctly — \"false\" won't match because the word boundary after \"ls\" fails when followed by \"e\", and \"ls-test\" would match but replacing \"ls\" with \"ls\" is harmless since it's a no-op.\n\nNow I'm tracing through what happens with a full path like `/tmp/nonexistent-for-ls-test` — the regex engine scans left-to-right, and `(?:/\\S+/)` would try to match from the initial \"/\" up to the \"/\" before \"ls\", capturing `/tmp/nonexistent-for-`. I'm overthinking the regex pattern matching for error messages—let me just move forward with the implementation. The normalizer only really needs to handle the unrecognized option case where it replaces both instances of \"gls\" with \"ls\", and I can avoid test paths that would complicate the pattern. Now I need to check the function signature for `format.Columns` to make sure I'm calling it correctly.\n\nLooking at the imports, all the functions I'm using are properly accessible: `format.Columns` and `format.PadRight` from the format package, `sys.IsTerminal`, `sys.TerminalWidth`, and `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler` from the sys package. Everything checks out.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering whether the `Columns` function is actually needed for the R1.1-R1.4 requirements. The diff tests run in a pipe so they won't exercise the multi-column code path, but since the requirement explicitly asks for multi-column output when stdout is a TTY, I need to implement it. The function fills entries top-to-bottom, left-to-right like GNU ls does, and my `writeColumns` function will call it to format the grid properly.\n\nI can't really verify the multi-column formatting works correctly through diff tests since they don't run in a TTY, but I'm going to trust that if the underlying `Columns` function is tested separately and my padding logic is correct, the output should match GNU ls. Time to stop overthinking and just write the code.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1598,"cache_read_input_tokens":29673,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1598},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e03b2217-b27c-48b9-99c4-00bd36989e1f","uuid":"976f114d-8f0b-40bf-b807-bba50396eed2"}
